<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>7.2.5: Practice</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01765</md:content-id>
    <md:title>7.2.5: Practice</md:title>
    <md:uuid>e8a62bb1-a7ef-41e8-a277-d4037867846b</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><para>Complete the following questions to practice the skills you have learned in this lesson.</para>
<div class="os-raise-ib-pset" data-button-text="Check" data-content-id="c305eb60-f955-4d60-b8ca-09013aef98d6" data-fire-learning-opportunity-event="eventnameY" data-fire-success-event="eventnameX" data-retry-limit="2" data-schema-version="1.0">
  <!--1a-->
  <exercise data-content-id="d58381a9-a116-4e2c-81b3-94b5b3cd37d1" data-problem-comparator="integer" data-problem-type="input" data-solution="16">
    <problem>
      <para>Here are two patterns of dots.</para>
      <para>Pattern A</para>
      <para><figure><media alt="Four figures, labeled step 0 through 3. Step 0, no dots. Step 1, row of 4 dots. Step 2, 2 rows of 4 dots each. Step 3, 3 rows of 4 dots each."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/3c9a638d3d94a27279cdb7074d8c6646bb9233b2"/></media></figure></para>
      <para>Pattern B</para>
      <para><figure><media alt="Four figures, step 0 through 3. Step 0, 2 dots. Step 1 row of 1 dot above a row of 2 dots. Step 2, 2 rows of 2 dots each, above a row of 2 dots. Step 3, 3 rows of 3 dots each, above row of 2 dots."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/09162edcf5ac5e0e874b3d0a4bf64423e00003e7"/></media></figure></para>
      <para> How many dots will there be in Step 4 of Pattern A?</para>
        <para> Enter your answer here:</para>
    </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! There will be 4 rows and 4 columns in Step 4.</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activity 7.2.2. </para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--1b-->
  <exercise data-content-id="bedc6d9a-4d0e-4890-901f-10b2428b8966" data-problem-comparator="integer" data-problem-type="input" data-solution="18">
    <problem>
      <para> How many dots will there be in Step 4 of Pattern B?</para>
        <para> Enter your answer here:</para>
    </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! The relationship is \(n^2+2\).</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activity 7.2.2. </para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--1c-->
  <exercise data-content-id="78ee8dd0-f16f-48fb-9102-636e1d540108" data-problem-type="dropdown" data-retry-limit="1" data-solution="Pattern B" data-solution-options="[&quot;Pattern A&quot;, &quot;Pattern B&quot;]">
    <problem>
      <para> Which pattern shows a quadratic relationship between the step number and the number of dots?</para>
        </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! The relationship is \(n^2+2\).</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activities 7.2.3 and 7.2.4. </para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--q2-->
  <exercise data-content-id="5df82e90-55ae-44d5-ad83-882333ba247f" data-problem-type="multiselect" data-solution="[&quot;\\(n^2\\)&quot;, &quot;\\(n \\cdot n\\)&quot;]" data-solution-options="[&quot;\\(n^2\\)&quot;, &quot;\\(2n\\)&quot;, &quot;\\(n \\cdot n\\)&quot;, &quot;\\(n+n\\)&quot;, &quot;\\(n+2\\)&quot;, &quot;\\(n \\div 2\\)&quot;]">
    <problem>
      <para>Each expression represents the total number of dots in a pattern where \(n\) represents the step number.</para>
      <para><emphasis>Select two</emphasis> expressions that represent a quadratic relationship between the step number and the total number of dots. (If you get stuck, consider sketching the first few steps of each pattern as described by the expression.)</para>
    </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct!</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activities 7.2.3 and 7.2.4.
      </para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--3a-->
  <exercise data-content-id="2937ceb9-af8f-40b1-8969-71308b1f2a66" data-problem-comparator="integer" data-problem-type="input" data-solution="32">
    <problem>
      <para>Use the pattern below to answer the questions.</para>
      <para><figure><media alt="Insert Alt Text"><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/951371a3b6b1037f7931fc4c9383339fea0673c6"/></media></figure></para>
      <para> How many blocks are in Step 4? </para>
        <para> Enter your answer here: </para>
    </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! The length is always \(n+2\), the width is always \(n+2\), and then \(n\) blocks are subtracted.
      </para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activities 7.3.2 and 7.3.4. 
      </para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--3b-->
  <exercise data-content-id="6e40b6dc-235c-482f-a9cc-eeada42e60f2" data-problem-comparator="integer" data-problem-type="input" data-solution="44">
    <problem>
      <para> How many blocks are in Step 5?</para>
        <para> Enter your answer here: </para>
    </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! The length is always \(n+2\), the width is always \(n+2\), and then \(n\) blocks are subtracted.
      </para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activities 7.3.2 and 7.3.4. 
      </para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--3c-->
  <exercise data-content-id="fdbe403b-0f9a-4799-ae16-bfce47321e47" data-problem-type="multiplechoice" data-retry-limit="3" data-solution="\((n+2)^2-n\)" data-solution-options="[&quot;\\(n^2-2\\)&quot;, &quot;\\(n+6\\)&quot;, &quot;\\((n+2)^2-n\\)&quot;, &quot;\\((n+2)^2\\)&quot;]">
    <problem>
      <para> How many blocks are in Step \(n\)?</para>
        </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! The length is always \(n+2\), the width is \(n+2\), and then \(n\) blocks are subtracted.</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activities 7.3.2 – 7.3.4. </para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--Do not edit below this line-->
  <solution> </solution>
  <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response"> </div>
</div></section>
</content>
